    {
      "name": "a link with NO bounds is the caller's shape error, not the record's contradiction",
      "$comment": "The authority-step totality guard. The step reaches isWithin(link.bounds, link.parentBounds) with both undefined and Object.keys() throws, violating the module's stated totality over its inputs. The walk already screens this upstream (walkableBounds: \"or isWithin would crash mid-walk\") and refuses it as malformed-authority-chain; the step must agree, so walk and step stay categorically aligned. not-attempted rather than failed for the same reason a non-finite depth is: a caller's shape corruption says nothing about whether the RECORD contradicts itself, and reporting it as failed would impeach a record to TC-0 over the caller's mistake. A well-formed readout always carries objects — the walk supplies parentBounds {} for a root link.",
      "input": {
        "chain": [
          {
            "parentDelegable": true
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected": {
        "status": "not-attempted",
        "depth": "malformed-authority-chain"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a link whose bounds is a non-object is malformed on the same rule",
      "$comment": "The totality guard's typed-corruption arm: bounds present but not an object. Object.keys(\"\") does not throw, so this arm would silently read as an EMPTY bounds — unbounded — and a forged-widening check against it would answer on nonsense rather than refusing.",
      "input": {
        "chain": [
          {
            "parentDelegable": true,
            "bounds": "unbounded",
            "parentBounds": {}
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected": {
        "status": "not-attempted",
        "depth": "malformed-authority-chain"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a link whose parentBounds is null is malformed — null is an object to typeof, and crashes",
      "$comment": "The totality guard's null arm. typeof null === \"object\", so a guard written on typeof alone still reaches Object.keys(null) and throws. Pinned separately so the fix cannot be written as a typeof check.",
      "input": {
        "chain": [
          {
            "parentDelegable": true,
            "bounds": {},
            "parentBounds": null
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected": {
        "status": "not-attempted",
        "depth": "malformed-authority-chain"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a link whose bounds is an ARRAY is malformed — Object.keys answers indices, not a refusal",
      "$comment": "The totality guard's array arm. typeof [] === \"object\" and [] !== null, so a guard written on those two terms alone admits an array — and Object.keys([]) answers [] rather than throwing, so the array would read as a bounds with NO dimensions, i.e. unbounded, and the forged-widening check would answer on it. Pinned separately because it is the only input that distinguishes the Array.isArray term from the rest of the guard.",
      "input": {
        "chain": [
          {
            "parentDelegable": true,
            "bounds": [],
            "parentBounds": {}
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected": {
        "status": "not-attempted",
        "depth": "malformed-authority-chain"
      }
    },
